# /confer-client-onboard — Scaffold a new client engagement

You are a **Confer onboarding operator standing up a new client engagement**.
Your job is to create the isolated knowledge + auth scaffolding for a new
client: a dedicated gbrain source that does NOT federate into any other
client's brain, a per-agent OAuth client scoped to that source, the source
registration, and an infra-inventory record. This is a runbook — run the
commands deliberately, verify each step, and stop on any failure.

**ISOLATION IS THE WHOLE POINT.** A client engagement's brain must never be
readable from another client's agents. Every source you create here is
`--no-federated`, and the OAuth client you mint is scoped to exactly that
source. Federation is opt-in and out of scope for onboarding.

**Reference tone:** this skill mirrors `/setup-gbrain` and `/sync-gbrain` —
verify prerequisites, run idempotent CLI commands, print a verdict block. The
gbrain CLI is the source of truth; run `gbrain <cmd> --help` if a flag name has
drifted, and prefer what the CLI prints over what is written here.

---

## Step 0: Gather engagement parameters

Ask (AskUserQuestion per the preamble format) for, at minimum:
- **client slug** — short, kebab-case, unique (e.g. `acme`, `moxi`,
  `razor`). Becomes the source-id stem and the inventory key. Validate it
  against `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$` — reject anything else.
- **source-id** — defaults to the client slug (e.g. `acme`). This is the
  isolated gbrain source.
- **agent name** — the OpenClaw agent that will read this source (e.g. the
  client's project-lead agent). The OAuth client is minted for it.
- **scope** — default `read` for a read-only client agent; `read write` only
  if the agent ingests into the brain itself.

Before creating anything, confirm the source-id does not already exist (Step 1
short-circuits if it does — never clobber an existing client's source).

---

## Step 1: Pre-flight — confirm gbrain CLI + no collision

```bash
gbrain --version || { echo "gbrain CLI not installed — run /setup-gbrain first"; exit 1; }
# Collision check: a source with this id must NOT already exist.
gbrain sources list --json 2>/dev/null | grep -q "\"id\":\"<source-id>\"" \
  && echo "COLLISION: source <source-id> already exists — STOP, pick a new id" \
  || echo "ok: <source-id> is free"
```

If the source already exists, STOP and surface it — onboarding an existing
client over the top risks cross-contaminating an established brain.

---

## Step 2: Create the ISOLATED gbrain source

Create the source with federation explicitly OFF so it cannot be read from any
other client's federated view:

```bash
gbrain sources add <source-id> --no-federated
```

`--no-federated` is the isolation guarantee — without it the source would join
the default federated read set. Verify it landed isolated:

```bash
gbrain sources list --json 2>/dev/null \
  | grep -A3 "\"id\":\"<source-id>\"" \
  | grep -i 'federated' || echo "(no federated flag → isolated, as intended)"
```

---

## Step 3: Mint a per-agent OAuth client

Mint an OAuth client scoped to the new agent + source. This prints a
`client_id` and a `client_secret` ONCE:

```bash
# Verified against gbrain v0.41.26+ (2026-05-29). Name is POSITIONAL.
# --source binds the client to the isolated source (the whole point of isolation).
# --grant-types client_credentials + client_secret_post = a machine client.
# NOTE the flag is --scopes (plural); --scope silently does nothing.
gbrain auth register-client "<agent-name>" \
  --grant-types client_credentials \
  --scopes "<scope>" \
  --source "<source-id>" \
  --token-endpoint-auth-method client_secret_post
```

`<scope>` is a single space-separated string, e.g. `"agent read write"` (the
CLI splits it). Do NOT pass an interactive/PKCE client (no `--redirect-uri`) —
that yields `grant_types=authorization_code` with no secret, which cannot do
the `client_credentials` token exchange the fleet agents use.

**SECRET HANDLING — do not leak.** The printed `client_secret` is shown once.
- NEVER write the secret into any file in this repo, the inventory entry, the
  skill output transcript, or a commit. This skill must produce zero secret
  values in any tracked file.
- The secret belongs in the agent's host-side creds file
  `/etc/openclaw/<agent>.brain.env` (chmod 600) on the agent's server — the
  same file `confer-openclaw-gbrain-wire.sh` reads. Instruct the operator to
  place it there out-of-band (e.g. via the secret channel in
  `~/Documents/Yatin's Brain/Secrets/`), then wire the agent with
  `confer-openclaw-gbrain-wire.sh wire <agent>` on that host.
- Record only the non-secret mapping (name, tier, source grant) in the access
  registry (Step 5) — not the secret, and not the raw client_id.

If `register-client` is not available in the installed gbrain build, surface
the `gbrain auth --help` output and STOP — do not improvise a token.

---

## Step 4: Register / seed the source content (optional, deferred)

The source now exists but is empty. Initial content ingest is a separate,
deliberate step — do NOT auto-ingest client material during onboarding. When
the client's docs are ready, ingest them with `/confer-brain-ingest` against
this `<source-id>` (it embeds so semantic search works). Note this as the
recommended next step; do not run it here.

---

## Step 5: Register the connection in the access registry

Record the engagement in the **durable access registry** so the fleet has a
machine-readable, non-secret map of which connection reads which source — and so
the drift-checker stops flagging it as an UNDECLARED connection. The registry is
`~/Agent-Ops/inventory/access.json` (git-tracked, no secrets, no client_ids). Add
a principal entry capturing ONLY non-secret fields:

```jsonc
// append to the "principals" array in ~/Agent-Ops/inventory/access.json
{
  "name": "<agent-name>",        // = the OAuth client_name
  "kind": "client-agent",         // or employee-agent / fleet-agent / external-connector / tooling
  "tier": "T2",                   // client lane = one client source. See 35-GBrain/access-tiers.md
  "owner": "<client> (client)",
  "grant": ["<source-id>"],       // its federated_read, by source-name. T2 = exactly one client source.
  "write_source": "<source-id>",
  "scope": "<scope>",
  "compartments": ["client:<source-id>"],
  "status": "active"
}
```

Then verify the registry matches prod and the new connection is no longer
undeclared:

```bash
~/Agent-Ops/scripts/gbrain-access-pull.sh        # refresh the live snapshot
~/Agent-Ops/scripts/gbrain-access-reconcile.py   # must be ✅ clean (no UNDECLARED for <agent-name>)
git -C ~/Agent-Ops add inventory/access.json && git -C ~/Agent-Ops commit -m "access: onboard <client-slug>"
```

Policy + tier model: `~/Agent-Ops/35-GBrain/access-tiers.md`. Change procedure:
`~/Agent-Ops/35-GBrain/access-change-runbook.md`.

If the new agent should appear in the SP-10 fleet registry, also add it to
`resolver/agents.yaml` with `gbrain_sources: [<source-id>]` and the correct
`escalates_to` — that keeps the rendered fleet-roster and escalation-chain
blocks in sync across all fleet-aware skills. (Editing `agents.yaml` is an
explicit, reviewed change — propose it, don't silently commit it as part of
onboarding.)

---

## Step 6: Verdict block

Print a doctor-style status block (mirrors `/setup-gbrain` Step 10):

```
client onboard: <client-slug>

  gbrain CLI ......... OK    <version>
  Source created ..... OK    <source-id> (federated=false, ISOLATED)
  OAuth client ....... OK    client_id=<client_id> (secret handled out-of-band)
  Inventory entry .... OK    <where recorded>
  agents.yaml ........ N/A   (propose separately if agent joins the fleet)
  Next: place client_secret in /etc/openclaw/<agent>.brain.env on the agent
        host, then run confer-openclaw-gbrain-wire.sh wire <agent>.
        Ingest client docs with /confer-brain-ingest <source-id> <dir>.
```

Any RED row gets a one-line next action.

---

## Important rules

- **Isolation-first.** Every source created here is `--no-federated`. Never
  add a new client source to the federated read set during onboarding.
- **Zero secrets in tracked files.** The OAuth `client_secret` never touches
  this repo, the inventory, or any commit. Only non-secret descriptive fields
  (name, tier, source grant) are recorded — never the secret, and not the raw
  client_id (it lives only in prod + the gitignored live snapshot).
- **No collision clobber.** Never re-create an existing source-id.
- **Don't auto-ingest.** Onboarding scaffolds; content ingest is a separate,
  deliberate `/confer-brain-ingest` run.

## Status reporting

End with a Completion Status (per the preamble protocol):
- **DONE** — isolated source created and verified non-federated, OAuth client
  minted (secret handled out-of-band), inventory entry recorded, next steps
  printed.
- **DONE_WITH_CONCERNS** — source + client created, but a follow-up is
  outstanding (e.g. `agents.yaml` edit pending review, or the secret still
  needs placing on the agent host). List what remains.
- **BLOCKED** — gbrain CLI missing, `register-client` unavailable in this
  build, or a source-id collision. State the blocker.
- **NEEDS_CONTEXT** — client slug / source-id / agent name missing or invalid.
